---
import type { ProtocolView } from './protocol-data';
interface Props { topicId: string; view: ProtocolView; }
const { topicId, view } = Astro.props;
---

{topicId === 'networking' && view.id === 'packet-journey' && <div class="visual-network visual-network-journey" aria-label="DNS lookup precedes the packet path; the packet then crosses client, local link, gateway, translation edge, and server">
  <div class="network-lookup" data-visual-node="0"><span class="visual-label">SIDE LOOKUP · DNS</span><strong>app.example.test</strong><span class="network-lookup-wire" aria-hidden="true">⇢ query · A record ⇠</span><strong>10.20.10.53</strong><small>answer 203.0.113.80</small></div>
  <div class="network-path"><div class="network-station" data-visual-node="1"><span class="station-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌘</span><strong>Client</strong><small>10.20.10.25</small></div><span class="network-wire" data-visual-node="2" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="network-station" data-visual-node="2"><span class="station-glyph" aria-hidden="true">▤</span><strong>Local link</strong><small>gateway MAC</small></div><span class="network-wire" data-visual-node="3" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="network-station" data-visual-node="3"><span class="station-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◇</span><strong>Gateway</strong><small>10.20.10.1</small></div><span class="network-wire" data-visual-node="4" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="network-station network-edge" data-visual-node="4"><span class="station-glyph" aria-hidden="true">↹</span><strong>Edge NAT</strong><small>source tuple may change</small></div><span class="network-wire" data-visual-node="5" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="network-station" data-visual-node="5"><span class="station-glyph" aria-hidden="true">▥</span><strong>Server</strong><small>203.0.113.80:443</small></div></div>
  <div class="network-envelope"><span class="visual-label">FIRST HOP ENVELOPE</span><div><span>ETHERNET <strong>gateway MAC</strong></span><span>IP <strong>203.0.113.80</strong></span><span>TCP <strong>51514 → 443</strong></span></div><small>Ethernet names this link’s next hop; IP names the remote destination.</small></div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'networking' && view.id === 'encapsulation' && <div class="visual-network visual-network-anatomy" aria-label="Ethernet frame encloses IP packet, which encloses TCP segment and application data">
  <div class="envelope-shell" data-visual-node="3"><span>ETHERNET FRAME <small>02:11:22:33:44:25 → gateway 02:aa:bb:cc:dd:01</small></span><div class="envelope-ip" data-visual-node="2"><span>IPV4 PACKET <small>10.20.10.25 → 203.0.113.80</small></span><div class="envelope-tcp" data-visual-node="1"><span>TCP SEGMENT <small>51514 → 443</small></span><div class="envelope-app" data-visual-node="0">HTTPS request bytes</div></div></div><small class="envelope-trailer">frame trailer · current link only</small></div>
  <div class="visual-note">At the router, the incoming frame is removed and a new frame is built for the next link. The IP destination normally remains the remote peer.</div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'networking' && view.id === 'cidr' && <div class="visual-network visual-network-cidr" aria-label="A twenty-four bit prefix makes 10.20.10.0 through 10.20.10.255 the connected IPv4 subnet">
  <div class="cidr-address" data-visual-node="0"><span class="visual-label">CLIENT ADDRESS</span><strong>10.20.10.25<span>/24</span></strong></div>
  <div class="cidr-bits" data-visual-node="1"><span class="visual-label">PREFIX / HOST BITS</span><div><strong>10 . 20 . 10</strong><strong>. 25</strong></div><div class="cidr-bit-rail"><i></i><i></i></div><small>24 network bits</small><small>8 host bits</small></div>
  <div class="cidr-peers"><div data-visual-node="2"><span>ON THIS SUBNET</span><strong>10.20.10.80</strong><small>may use local neighbor resolution</small></div><div data-visual-node="3"><span>OUTSIDE THIS SUBNET</span><strong>203.0.113.80</strong><small>needs a selected route / next hop</small></div></div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'networking' && view.id === 'arp' && <div class="visual-network visual-network-arp" aria-label="ARP asks for gateway address on the local link; the resulting Ethernet frame uses gateway MAC while keeping remote IP destination">
  <div class="arp-peers"><div data-visual-node="0"><span class="visual-label">CLIENT</span><strong>10.20.10.25</strong><small>route via 10.20.10.1</small></div><div class="arp-message"><div data-visual-node="1">ARP request → <small>Who has 10.20.10.1?</small></div><div data-visual-node="2">← ARP reply <small>02:aa:bb:cc:dd:01</small></div></div><div data-visual-node="2"><span class="visual-label">GATEWAY</span><strong>10.20.10.1</strong><small>02:aa:bb:cc:dd:01</small></div></div>
  <div class="arp-frame" data-visual-node="3"><span>OUTGOING ETHERNET FRAME</span><div><strong>Dst MAC · gateway</strong><strong>Dst IP · 203.0.113.80</strong></div><small>The remote server’s MAC is not present on this first link.</small></div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'networking' && view.id === 'forwarding' && <div class="visual-network visual-network-route" aria-label="Route lookup selects the longest matching prefix before neighbor resolution and optional NAT">
  <div class="route-query"><span class="visual-label">DESTINATION</span><strong>203.0.113.80</strong><small>Which prefix matches most specifically?</small></div>
  <div class="route-table"><span class="visual-label">CLIENT ROUTING TABLE</span><div><code>10.20.10.0/24</code><span>local · eth0</span></div><div data-visual-node="0"><code>203.0.113.64/26</code><span>via 10.20.10.1</span></div><div><code>203.0.113.0/24</code><span>via 10.20.10.253</span></div><div><code>0.0.0.0/0</code><span>via 10.20.10.254</span></div></div>
  <div class="route-result"><span class="visual-label">SELECTED IN THIS SCENARIO</span><strong data-visual-node="0">/26 → 10.20.10.1</strong><span data-visual-node="1">resolve that next hop’s MAC</span><span data-visual-node="2">build outgoing frame</span><span data-visual-node="3">NAT only if configured at a later edge</span></div>
  <p class="visual-note">This table deliberately includes overlapping routes: <code>203.0.113.80</code> matches the <code>/26</code>, <code>/24</code>, and default, so the <code>/26</code> wins.</p>
</div>}

{topicId === 'networking' && view.id === 'tcp' && <div class="visual-network visual-network-tcp" aria-label="TCP SYN, SYN-ACK and ACK precede application stream data">
  <div class="tcp-peers"><span>CLIENT · 10.20.10.25:51514</span><span>SERVER · 203.0.113.80:443</span></div><div class="tcp-sequence"><div data-visual-node="0"><strong>SYN</strong><span>→</span><small>open connection</small></div><div data-visual-node="1"><strong>SYN-ACK</strong><span>←</span><small>server responds</small></div><div data-visual-node="2"><strong>ACK</strong><span>→</span><small>handshake complete</small></div><div data-visual-node="3"><strong>DATA</strong><span>⇄</span><small>ordered byte stream</small></div></div><div class="tcp-udp-note"><strong>UDP comparison</strong><span>UDP has ports and checksums but no transport connection handshake or built-in stream retransmission.</span></div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'networking' && view.id === 'socket' && <div class="visual-network visual-network-socket" aria-label="TCP protocol plus source and destination addresses and ports identify the transport connection">
  <div class="socket-tuple"><div data-visual-node="0"><span>PROTOCOL</span><strong>TCP</strong></div><div data-visual-node="1"><span>SOURCE IP</span><strong>10.20.10.25</strong></div><div data-visual-node="1"><span>SOURCE PORT</span><strong>51514</strong></div><div data-visual-node="2"><span>DESTINATION IP</span><strong>203.0.113.80</strong></div><div data-visual-node="2"><span>DESTINATION PORT</span><strong>443</strong></div></div><div class="socket-listener" data-visual-node="3"><span>SERVER SOCKET</span><strong>sshd or web server binds an address and port</strong><small>This HTTPS example requires a TCP listener on :443.</small></div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'networking' && view.id === 'nat' && <div class="visual-network visual-network-nat" aria-label="NAT rewrites the source IP and port at the edge, keeps mapping state, and reverses it for matching reply traffic">
  <div class="nat-tuples"><div data-visual-node="0"><span>INSIDE · BEFORE</span><strong>10.20.10.25:51514</strong><small>→ 203.0.113.80:443</small></div><span class="nat-transform" data-visual-node="1">SNAT →</span><div data-visual-node="2"><span>OUTSIDE · AFTER</span><strong>198.51.100.25:62001</strong><small>→ 203.0.113.80:443</small></div></div><div class="nat-mapping" data-visual-node="1"><span>EDGE MAPPING STATE</span><strong>private source ↔ translated source</strong><small>Needed to identify the matching reply flow</small></div><div class="nat-reply" data-visual-node="3">← reply to 198.51.100.25:62001 · reverse map to 10.20.10.25:51514</div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'networking' && view.id === 'dns' && <div class="visual-network visual-network-dns" aria-label="DNS resolver may answer from cache or follow delegated authority, returning an address before the application packet is sent">
  <div class="dns-query" data-visual-node="0"><span>APPLICATION</span><strong>app.example.test?</strong></div><div class="dns-split"><div data-visual-node="1"><span>CACHE</span><strong>valid A answer?</strong><small>TTL controls reuse</small></div><div data-visual-node="2"><span>DNS HIERARCHY</span><strong>delegation → authority</strong><small>query only when needed</small></div></div><div class="dns-answer" data-visual-node="3"><span>RESOLVER ANSWER</span><strong>203.0.113.80</strong><small>Now the application can attempt transport.</small></div><div class="dns-boundary">DNS lookup is beside the later packet path, not a hop in it.</div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'networking' && view.id === 'firewall' && <div class="visual-network visual-network-firewall" aria-label="A firewall checks a new SYN against policy, records allowed state, and evaluates return traffic using that state if configured">
  <div class="firewall-flow"><div data-visual-node="0"><span>NEW FLOW</span><strong>TCP SYN → :443</strong></div><div class="firewall-gate" data-visual-node="1"><span>POLICY GATE</span><strong>allow new connection?</strong></div><div data-visual-node="2"><span>RETURN FLOW</span><strong>state match → allow</strong></div></div><div class="firewall-compare"><div><strong>STATEFUL</strong><small>can match allowed connection state</small></div><div><strong>STATELESS</strong><small>each packet needs an applicable rule</small></div></div><div class="firewall-drop" data-visual-node="3">DENIED OR UNMATCHED · stop at this policy boundary</div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'networking' && view.id === 'load-balancer' && <div class="visual-network visual-network-lb" aria-label="L4 balancer chooses using connection metadata; L7 HTTP balancer can route by host or path after protocol inspection">
  <div class="lb-path"><div data-visual-node="0"><span>L4 EVIDENCE</span><strong>IP · port · protocol</strong><small>TCP connection metadata</small></div><div data-visual-node="1"><span>L4 CHOICE</span><strong>eligible backend</strong><small>connection-oriented routing</small></div></div><div class="lb-path"><div data-visual-node="2"><span>L7 EVIDENCE</span><strong>Host + /api path</strong><small>HTTP attributes after inspection</small></div><div data-visual-node="3"><span>L7 CHOICE</span><strong>healthy API target</strong><small>request-oriented routing</small></div></div><p class="visual-note">The balancer’s layer determines the information it can inspect. A healthy target set remains necessary at either layer.</p>
</div>}

{topicId === 'networking' && view.id === 'diagnose' && <div class="visual-network visual-network-diagnose" aria-label="Troubleshooting ladder finds the first failed network assumption">
  <div class="diagnose-rail">{view.stages.map((stage, i) => <div data-visual-node={i}><span>{String(i + 1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><strong>{stage.label}</strong><small>{stage.detail}</small></div>)}</div>
  <div class="diagnose-evidence"><span class="visual-label">FIRST FAILED ASSUMPTION</span><strong>Follow the evidence, not the timeout alone.</strong><p>Successful DNS does not prove TCP delivery. A successful direct IP connection does not prove the name or proxy path.</p></div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'containers' && view.id === 'assemble' && <div class="visual-containers visual-container-assemble" aria-label="One Linux host process gains namespace views, image root filesystem, cgroup controls, and security policy while still sharing the host kernel">
  <div class="host-processes"><span class="visual-label">HOST PROCESS VIEW</span><div><code>1</code><span>systemd</span></div><div><code>921</code><span>sshd</span></div><div class="host-process-current"><code>18472</code><strong>node server.js</strong></div><p>Same process is scheduled by the host kernel.</p></div>
  <div class="container-boundary"><span class="visual-label">CONTAINER EXECUTION CONTEXT</span><div class="boundary-namespace" data-visual-node="1"><span>NAMESPACES · isolated views</span><strong>PID 1 inside / PID 18472 on host</strong><small>mount · network · PID · UTS · IPC</small></div><div class="boundary-rootfs" data-visual-node="2"><span>ROOT FILESYSTEM</span><strong>image layers + writable state</strong></div><div class="boundary-cgroup" data-visual-node="3"><span>CGROUP</span><strong>memory / CPU accounting and limits</strong><div class="resource-meter"><i></i></div></div><div class="boundary-security" data-visual-node="4"><span>SECURITY POLICY</span><strong>capabilities · seccomp · LSM</strong></div><div class="boundary-process" data-visual-node="0"><strong>node server.js</strong><small>one Linux process · two PID views</small></div></div>
  <div class="kernel-rail"><span>LINUX HOST KERNEL</span><strong>shared by host processes and containers</strong></div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'containers' && view.id === 'namespaces' && <div class="visual-containers visual-container-namespaces" aria-label="The same process has host PID 18472 and container PID 1, plus distinct mount and network views">
  <div class="namespace-host" data-visual-node="0"><span>HOST VIEW</span><strong>PID 18472 · node server.js</strong><small>scheduled by Linux host kernel</small></div><div class="namespace-lenses"><div data-visual-node="1"><span>PID LENS</span><strong>PID 1 inside</strong><small>same process, different numbering</small></div><div data-visual-node="2"><span>MOUNT LENS</span><strong>/srv/app rootfs</strong><small>different mounts and root path</small></div><div data-visual-node="3"><span>NETWORK LENS</span><strong>eth0 · container IP</strong><small>its own interfaces, routes, localhost</small></div></div><div class="namespace-kernel">ONE SHARED HOST KERNEL</div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'containers' && view.id === 'cgroups' && <div class="visual-containers visual-container-cgroups" aria-label="The kernel places processes in a cgroup, accounts for resource usage, applies optional limits, and reacts to memory pressure">
  <div class="cgroup-process" data-visual-node="0"><span>PROCESS GROUP</span><strong>node server.js + workers</strong></div><div class="cgroup-gauges"><div data-visual-node="1"><span>CPU USE</span><strong>accounted</strong><i></i></div><div data-visual-node="2"><span>MEMORY</span><strong>current / configured max</strong><i></i></div></div><div class="cgroup-pressure" data-visual-node="3"><span>PRESSURE EVENT</span><strong>reclaim · throttle · possible OOM</strong><small>What happens depends on controller, kernel state, and configuration.</small></div><p class="visual-note">The container remains a container even if no runtime memory ceiling was configured.</p>
</div>}

{topicId === 'containers' && view.id === 'build-cache' && <div class="visual-containers visual-container-cache" aria-label="Dockerfile source edit invalidates the source-copy step but lets earlier dependency install work remain cached when its inputs did not change">
  <div class="cache-dockerfile"><span>DOCKERFILE</span><code>FROM node:22</code><code>COPY package*.json ./</code><code>RUN npm ci</code><code>COPY server.js ./</code></div><div class="cache-status"><span>AFTER EDITING server.js</span><div data-visual-node="0"><strong>BASE</strong><small>CACHED</small></div><div data-visual-node="1"><strong>PACKAGE FILES</strong><small>CACHED</small></div><div data-visual-node="2"><strong>INSTALL</strong><small>CACHED</small></div><div data-visual-node="3"><strong>SOURCE</strong><small>REBUILT</small></div></div><p class="visual-note">Move <code>COPY . .</code> before install and a source edit can invalidate the expensive dependency step.</p>
</div>}

{topicId === 'containers' && view.id === 'image' && <div class="visual-containers visual-container-image" aria-label="Read-only image layers are shared input while a container gets its own writable layer and optional persistent volume">
  <div class="image-stack"><span class="visual-label">IMAGE · READ-ONLY INPUT</span><div data-visual-node="0">base runtime <small>node:22</small></div><div data-visual-node="1">dependencies <small>npm ci</small></div><div data-visual-node="2">application <small>server.js</small></div><div class="writable-layer" data-visual-node="3">container writable state <small>per container</small></div></div>
  <div class="image-fan"><span class="visual-label">THREE RUNNING INSTANCES</span><div><strong>web-a</strong><small>its own writes</small></div><div><strong>web-b</strong><small>separate writes</small></div><div><strong>web-c</strong><small>separate writes</small></div><div class="persistent-volume" data-visual-node="4"><strong>app-data volume</strong><small>lifecycle separate from web-a</small></div></div>
  <p class="visual-note">Removing <code>web-a</code> removes its container-local writes. It does not mutate the image or the other containers; an explicitly managed volume can persist.</p>
</div>}

{topicId === 'containers' && view.id === 'run' && <div class="visual-containers visual-container-run" aria-label="Docker resolves the image, creates runtime state, attaches networking, starts PID 1, and optionally publishes a host port">
  <div class="run-command"><span class="visual-label">REQUEST</span><code>docker run -p 8080:3000 demo-api:1.0</code></div><div class="run-lanes"><div><span>DOCKER / RUNTIME</span>{view.stages.slice(0,3).map((stage,i)=><p data-visual-node={i}>{stage.label}<small>{stage.detail}</small></p>)}</div><div><span>CONTAINER</span>{view.stages.slice(3).map((stage,i)=><p data-visual-node={i+3}>{stage.label}<small>{stage.detail}</small></p>)}</div></div><div class="run-port"><span>HOST :8080</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>CONTAINER :3000</span><small>Port publication is configured; a container port alone does not publish a host listener.</small></div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'containers' && view.id === 'container-packet' && <div class="visual-containers visual-container-packet" aria-label="A client reaches a published host port, host forwarding sends it through a container attachment, and the app must listen on the container address">
  <div class="container-packet-path"><div data-visual-node="0"><span>CLIENT</span><strong>host:8080</strong></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><div data-visual-node="1"><span>HOST RULE</span><strong>:8080 → :3000</strong></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><div data-visual-node="2"><span>ATTACHMENT</span><strong>container network</strong></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><div data-visual-node="3"><span>PROCESS</span><strong>node :3000</strong></div></div><div class="packet-bind"><span>LISTENER SCOPE</span><strong>0.0.0.0:3000 inside container</strong><small>Loopback-only 127.0.0.1:3000 inside the namespace is a different bind.</small></div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'containers' && view.id === 'boundary' && <div class="visual-containers visual-container-lifecycle" aria-label="cgroup limit and stop signal affect the running process; the grace period may end in forced termination">
  <div class="lifecycle-process"><span class="visual-label">PID NAMESPACE</span><strong>PID 1 · node server.js</strong><div><span>PID 8 · worker</span><span>PID 9 · worker</span></div></div><div class="lifecycle-meter" data-visual-node="0"><span>CGROUP MEMORY</span><strong>usage compared with configured limit</strong><i></i></div><div class="lifecycle-time">{view.stages.slice(1).map((stage,i)=><div data-visual-node={i+1}><span>{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><strong>{stage.label}</strong><small>{stage.detail}</small></div>)}</div><p class="visual-note">A running container is a running process lifecycle. Graceful exit depends on the application handling the configured stop signal and its children.</p>
</div>}

{topicId === 'containers' && view.id === 'security-boundary' && <div class="visual-containers visual-container-security" aria-label="Namespaces, capability reduction, syscall filtering and mount policy form distinct security controls around a Linux process">
  <div class="security-process"><strong>node server.js</strong><small>untrusted input can reach this process</small></div><div class="security-rings"><div data-visual-node="0"><span>NAMESPACE VIEW</span><small>which resources are visible?</small></div><div data-visual-node="1"><span>CAPABILITIES</span><small>which privileged operations?</small></div><div data-visual-node="2"><span>SECCOMP / LSM</span><small>which syscalls and accesses?</small></div><div data-visual-node="3"><span>HOST MOUNTS</span><small>which host paths can be touched?</small></div></div><div class="security-warning">Privileged mode or broad writable host mounts can reopen paths these controls were meant to close.</div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'containers' && view.id === 'registry' && <div class="visual-containers visual-container-registry" aria-label="Builder produces content-addressed image layers, tag points to a digest, registry stores content, and another host pulls before run">
  <div class="registry-flow">{view.stages.map((stage,i)=><div data-visual-node={i}><span>{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><strong>{stage.label}</strong><small>{stage.detail}</small></div>)}</div><div class="registry-identity"><span>TAG · demo-api:1.0</span><strong>Digest · sha256:4ad7…91c2</strong><small>Tag can move; digest names content.</small></div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'containers' && view.id === 'break-fix' && <div class="visual-containers visual-container-breakfix" aria-label="A running process does not prove a listening service; diagnostics check process, listener, published port, resources, and storage in order">
  <div class="breakfix-path">{view.stages.map((stage,i)=><div data-visual-node={i}><span>{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><strong>{stage.label}</strong><small>{stage.detail}</small></div>)}</div><div class="breakfix-trap"><span>EXAMPLE TRAP</span><strong>Container running · callers time out</strong><small>Application bound only to 127.0.0.1 inside the namespace.</small></div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'containers' && view.id === 'vm-compare' && <div class="visual-containers visual-container-vm" aria-label="Container processes share host kernel; a VM supplies a guest kernel over virtual hardware and can itself run containers">
  <div class="vm-model"><div data-visual-node="0"><strong>CONTAINER</strong><span>app process</span><span>namespaces + cgroups</span><small>shared HOST KERNEL</small></div><div data-visual-node="2"><strong>VIRTUAL MACHINE</strong><span>app process</span><span>guest operating system</span><small>GUEST KERNEL</small></div></div><div class="vm-together" data-visual-node="3">CLOUD HOST → VM → many containers → one guest kernel</div><p class="visual-note">The layers are often combined; this comparison shows kernel ownership, not a blanket security rating.</p>
</div>}

{topicId === 'ssh' && view.id === 'trust' && <div class="visual-ssh visual-ssh-trust" aria-label="Client and server exchange SSH transport messages; host private key stays on server and the client checks known_hosts">
  <div class="ssh-peer"><span class="visual-label">CLIENT · laptop-01</span><strong>ssh alice@server.example</strong><div class="ssh-local-file"><span>known_hosts</span><small>trusted host public key record</small></div><div class="ssh-secret"><span>USER PRIVATE KEY</span><small>stays local · used later</small></div></div>
  <div class="ssh-rail"><span class="visual-label">TRANSPORT TRACE</span>{view.stages.map((stage,i)=><div data-visual-node={i}><span>{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><strong>{stage.label}</strong><small>{stage.detail}</small><i aria-hidden="true">{i % 2 ? '←' : '→'}</i></div>)}<div class="ssh-wire-clear">PRE-NEWKEYS · host proof and algorithm messages</div></div>
  <div class="ssh-peer"><span class="visual-label">SERVER · server.example</span><strong>sshd :22</strong><div class="ssh-secret"><span>HOST PRIVATE KEY</span><small>signs exchange · stays server-side</small></div><div class="ssh-local-file"><span>host public key</span><small>sent with signed exchange</small></div></div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'ssh' && view.id === 'encrypted' && <div class="visual-ssh visual-ssh-crypto" aria-label="Public key agreement values cross the network, each peer derives secret locally, and NEWKEYS activates encrypted traffic">
  <div class="crypto-peers"><div><span class="visual-label">CLIENT</span><strong>ephemeral secret</strong><small>local only</small></div><div class="crypto-cross" data-visual-node="0">public agreement values <span>⇄</span></div><div><span class="visual-label">SERVER</span><strong>ephemeral secret</strong><small>local only</small></div></div><div class="crypto-derive" data-visual-node="1"><span>LOCAL DERIVATION</span><strong>both sides obtain shared key material</strong><small>finished session key is never sent as a packet</small></div><div class="crypto-newkeys" data-visual-node="2"><span>NEWKEYS</span><strong>switch to negotiated protection</strong></div><div class="crypto-tunnel" data-visual-node="3"><span>ENCRYPTED SSH TRANSPORT</span><i aria-hidden="true"></i><small>subsequent authentication and channel messages travel here</small></div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'ssh' && view.id === 'user-auth' && <div class="visual-ssh visual-ssh-auth" aria-label="User public key is offered to server, a local private key signs proof, and the server checks authorized_keys and verifies the signature">
  <div class="ssh-auth-side"><span class="visual-label">CLIENT</span><strong>alice</strong><div class="ssh-secret" data-visual-node="2">private key / agent <small>local SIGN operation</small></div></div><div class="ssh-auth-wire"><span data-visual-node="0">offer public key →</span><span data-visual-node="2">send signature →</span><span data-visual-node="3">← authentication accepted</span></div><div class="ssh-auth-side"><span class="visual-label">SERVER</span><strong>sshd</strong><div class="ssh-local-file" data-visual-node="1">authorized_keys <small>public key + policy</small></div><div class="ssh-verify" data-visual-node="3">VERIFY proof · authenticate alice</div></div><p class="visual-note">The encrypted transport already exists here. The user’s private key remains on the client or its local signing helper.</p>
</div>}

{topicId === 'ssh' && view.id === 'channels' && <div class="visual-ssh visual-ssh-channels" aria-label="One authenticated encrypted SSH connection can carry shell, command, SFTP, and forwarding channels">
  <div class="channel-head"><span>AUTHENTICATED CLIENT</span><span>SERVER · sshd</span></div><div class="channel-tunnel"><span class="visual-label">ONE ENCRYPTED TRANSPORT</span>{view.stages.map((stage,i)=><div data-visual-node={i}><strong>{stage.label}</strong><span>{stage.detail}</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i></div>)}</div><div class="channel-forward"><span>LOCAL FORWARD EXAMPLE</span><code>localhost:15432 → SSH → bastion → db.internal:5432</code></div><p class="visual-note">Forwarding opens a separate path from the server side to its destination. Login permission does not automatically grant forwarding permission.</p>
</div>}

{topicId === 'ssh' && view.id === 'ssh-forwarding' && <div class="visual-ssh visual-ssh-forwarding" aria-label="Local forwarding listens on client, remote forwarding listens on server, dynamic forwarding creates a client SOCKS listener, and ProxyJump routes to another SSH server">
  <div class="forwarding-forms"><div data-visual-node="0"><span>LOCAL · -L</span><strong>client listener</strong><small>→ SSH → server-side target</small></div><div data-visual-node="1"><span>REMOTE · -R</span><strong>server listener</strong><small>→ SSH → client-side target</small></div><div data-visual-node="2"><span>DYNAMIC · -D</span><strong>client SOCKS listener</strong><small>→ SSH → varying targets</small></div><div data-visual-node="3"><span>JUMP · -J</span><strong>client → bastion</strong><small>→ private SSH server</small></div></div><div class="forwarding-rail"><span>Which machine owns the listening port?</span><strong>That determines the direction of the forwarded connection.</strong></div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'ssh' && view.id === 'credential-tooling' && <div class="visual-ssh visual-ssh-tooling" aria-label="Private key remains local, agent may sign locally, agent forwarding grants remote access to a signing proxy, and CA certificate expresses signed identity">
  <div class="tooling-local" data-visual-node="0"><span>CLIENT PRIVATE KEY</span><strong>local signing authority</strong><small>never copied to the server by normal public-key authentication</small></div><div class="tooling-agent" data-visual-node="1"><span>ssh process → agent socket</span><strong>SIGN locally</strong><small>signature crosses the SSH transport</small></div><div class="tooling-forward" data-visual-node="2"><span>AGENT FORWARDING</span><strong>remote proxy socket can request signatures</strong><small>key stays local, authority is exposed to that remote environment</small></div><div class="tooling-ca" data-visual-node="3"><span>SSH CERTIFICATE</span><strong>CA-signed user or host identity</strong><small>servers or clients trust a configured CA</small></div>
</div>}

{topicId === 'ssh' && view.id === 'hardening' && <div class="visual-ssh visual-ssh-hardening" aria-label="Network, host identity, user authentication, and channel permissions are separate SSH policy gates">
  <div class="hardening-gates"><div data-visual-node="0"><span>BEFORE SSH</span><strong>network :22</strong><small>reachability and source controls</small></div><div data-visual-node="1"><span>TRANSPORT</span><strong>host key</strong><small>trusted server identity</small></div><div data-visual-node="2"><span>LOGIN</span><strong>user policy</strong><small>keys, methods, account access</small></div><div data-visual-node="3"><span>AFTER LOGIN</span><strong>channel policy</strong><small>shell, exec, forwarding</small></div></div><div class="hardening-config"><span>EFFECTIVE SERVER SETTINGS</span><code>PermitRootLogin no</code><code>PasswordAuthentication no</code><code>AllowTcpForwarding no</code></div><p class="visual-note">Each setting changes a different gate. Test the effective configuration and a working access path before changing a remote server.</p>
</div>}

{topicId === 'ssh' && view.id === 'ssh-diagnose' && <div class="visual-ssh visual-ssh-diagnose" aria-label="SSH diagnosis moves through name/TCP, algorithms, host key, user authentication, and channel request to find the first failure">
  <div class="ssh-failure-ladder">{view.stages.map((stage,i)=><div data-visual-node={i}><span>{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><strong>{stage.label}</strong><small>{stage.detail}</small></div>)}</div><div class="ssh-debug-trace"><span>CLIENT DEBUG · ILLUSTRATIVE</span><code>ssh -vvv alice@server.example</code><p>Timeout before SSH version exchange → inspect DNS/TCP. Changed host key → inspect server identity. Permission denied after NEWKEYS → inspect user authorization.</p></div>
</div>}
